# Musical consonance and the inversion of intervals. Two tones sound consonant when their frequencies stand in a simple
# whole-number ratio: the octave is two to one, the fifth three to two, the fourth four to three, the major third five to
# four, the minor third six to five. Intervals within the octave come in INVERSION pairs, an interval and the one that
# completes it to the octave: the fifth inverts to the fourth, the major third to the minor sixth, the minor third to the
# major sixth, the unison to the octave. This file MODELS the eight intervals of the diatonic set and DEFINES inversion as
# the map that completes each to the octave, then PROVES that inversion is an involution, inverting twice returns the
# original interval, so the pairing is perfect. It records, as machine-integer facts, that a fifth stacked on a fourth is
# an octave and a major third on a minor third is a fifth, and that the Pythagorean and syntonic commas are real gaps. The
# inversion theorem is quantified over the actual interval type and proved by induction.

# The eight intervals of the diatonic set, from unison up to the octave.
form interval
  case unison
  case minor-third
  case major-third
  case fourth
  case fifth
  case minor-sixth
  case major-sixth
  case octave

# Inversion within the octave: each interval is sent to the one that completes it to the octave. Unison and octave swap,
# the fifth and fourth swap, the major third and minor sixth swap, the minor third and major sixth swap.
task invert
  take i, like interval
  like interval
  fork case, read i
    case unison
      send back
        make octave
    case octave
      send back
        make unison
    case fifth
      send back
        make fourth
    case fourth
      send back
        make fifth
    case major-third
      send back
        make minor-sixth
    case minor-sixth
      send back
        make major-third
    case minor-third
      send back
        make major-sixth
    case major-sixth
      send back
        make minor-third

# INVERSION IS AN INVOLUTION: inverting an interval twice returns the original, invert (invert i) = i, for every interval.
# So inversion is a perfect pairing of the eight intervals, each with the interval that completes it to the octave. Proved
# by induction over the eight intervals.
rule inversion-is-an-involution
  seat i, like interval
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call invert
        call invert
          read i
      read i
  fold i

# A FIFTH STACKED ON A FOURTH IS AN OCTAVE, by multiplying the ratios: three halves times four thirds is two, checked by
# cross-multiplication, 3 * 4 = 2 * (2 * 3), both twelve.
hold fifth-on-fourth-is-an-octave
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 3
      code 4
    call multiply
      code 2
      call multiply
        code 2
        code 3

# A MAJOR THIRD STACKED ON A MINOR THIRD IS A FIFTH: five fourths times six fifths is three halves, cross-multiplied
# (5 * 6) * 2 = 3 * (4 * 5), both sixty.
hold major-third-on-minor-third-is-a-fifth
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      call multiply
        code 5
        code 6
      code 2
    call multiply
      code 3
      call multiply
        code 4
        code 5

# THE PYTHAGOREAN COMMA: twelve fifths, three to the twelfth, 531441, overshoot seven octaves, two to the nineteenth,
# 524288, so the comma is a real gap.
hold twelve-fifths-overshoot-seven-octaves
  call is-above
    code 531441
    code 524288

# THE SYNTONIC COMMA is eighty-one to eighty: three to the fourth, 81, above sixteen times five, 80.
hold syntonic-comma-is-eighty-one-to-eighty
  call is-above
    code 81
    call multiply
      code 16
      code 5
